WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.895 --> 00:00:03.110
Lex Megatrends.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.970
The Crypto Revolution.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.420
Anarchy has not conquered the
UK despite the predictions

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.740
of the Sex Pistols.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.940
But a version of it is gaining
traction in finance worldwide.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.960
Cryptocurrencies such as
Bitcoin have bedazzled investors

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.260
and are entering the
financial mainstream.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.710
Cryptos embody a core
tenet of anarchism,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:31.210
co-operation in the absence
of centralised authority.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.270
The financial
establishment is worried.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.230
In this video we predict
where it may all end.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.480
The invention of
Bitcoin in the noughties

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.980
is credited to the
pseudonymous Satoshi Nakamoto.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.640
He, or perhaps they, had
the revolutionary idea

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.700
of creating a
decentralised currency.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.790
Bitcoin deals are recorded
in a public distributed

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.580
ledger, a database
accessed and managed

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.890
by multiple participants.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:03.460
They verify transactions by
solving cryptographic puzzles

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.500
and are paid in Bitcoin.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.680
But Bitcoin is a
flagship with flaws.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.770
Criminals favour the currency,
which sometimes resembles

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.480
a Ponzi scheme.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.680
It is polluting.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:15.940
It is volatile.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.400
And Bitcoin is too
clumsy to be handy

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.380
for everyday transactions.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.640
Despite this, Bitcoin has done
something truly remarkable.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.550
It has shown that a
decentralised digital currency

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.360
can suck in billions, thanks
to healthy dissent, base

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.790
greed, lofty idealism, and
sheer fear of missing out.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.440
It was valued at over $1tn
in late October, 2021.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.490
The crypto universe was
valued at some $2.4tn.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.570
That compares with
the UK GDP of $2.7tn.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:53.420
Lex is still sceptical
about Bitcoin's value.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.340
We don't like European bank
stocks, or Brussels sprouts,

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.180
either.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.900
But we believe digital
assets will disrupt finance.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.300
Central banks fret
that cryptos could

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.820
destabilise financial systems.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.729
Bitcoin is 12
times more volatile

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:12.070
than the S&amp;P 500 index.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:14.950
A prolonged bear market
could bring it down to earth

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.270
with a bump.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.520
That would hurt
leveraged investors most.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.770
Stablecoins pegged to
official currencies

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.440
would increase, rather
than dampen risks,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.260
if assets and liabilities
were mismatched.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.650
Official currencies help
governments stay in charge,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.040
alongside police and armies.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.570
Establishments fear cryptos will
undermine their power, as well

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.250
as financial stability.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.580
That is why China has
launched a digital yuan

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.710
for retail transactions.

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:48.130
The European Union plans to
introduce a digital euro.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.650
Europeans could end up
depositing some of their cash

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.950
with the ECB, or
national central banks,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.650
such as the German Bundesbank.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.770
Central bankers are too
high and mighty to service

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:01.220
retail customers.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:05.110
Commercial banks would do
that for them, somehow.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.870
Digital euros or
dollars or pounds

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.080
wouldn't generate easy
profits for banks, though.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.620
Funding costs would go up
if their own deposits fell.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.310
In a crisis customers
might leave them in droves.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.640
Digital assets create
opportunities for banks, too.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.880
Many are hiring teams
of crypto experts

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.240
to advise clients and their
own senior management.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.100
Official regulation
of digital assets

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.320
will make their jobs easier.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.410
Eventually low-friction
dealing in stocks and bonds,

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.250
using public ledger technology,
will hit profits for some

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.260
but raise it for others.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.290
Lex believes the financial
establishment will struggle

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.300
to assimilate digital assets.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.610
But assimilate them it will.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.070
Some cryptos will stay
forever on the margins.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:56.050
But anarchism in finance,
as in politics and music,

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.390
is very vulnerable to sell-outs.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.440
As crypto bros take jobs in
the City and on Wall Street

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.450
the idealists may
ask one another

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.620
the question posed by Johnny
Rotten of the Sex Pistols:

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.000
"Ever get the feeling
you've been cheated?"

